  "reconstruction": {
    "text": "For every complete finite-dimensional real inner-product space E with its compatible Borel measurable structure and canonical Haar/Lebesgue measure λ = volume, every C² potential V : E → ℝ, and all real constants κ, η with κ ≥ 1 and η > 0, suppose that for every x, v ∈ E,\n    κ⁻¹ ‖v‖² ≤ D²V(x)[v,v] ≤ ‖v‖².\nLet d = dim_ℝ E, s = √η, μ = λ.tilted(-V), and\n    C = ((√(2πη))⁻¹)^d,\n    A(y) = C ∫_E exp(-V(x) - ‖y-x‖²/(2η)) λ(dx),\n    Vη(y) = -log(A(y)),\n    R_y = μ.tilted(x ↦ -‖x-y‖²/(2η)).\nThe tilt means actual normalized exponential reweighting of the indicated base measure, or the zero measure on its nonintegrable branch. Vη is defined using the literal unnormalized volume convolution above: the input potential V retains its additive normalization; no assumption ∫ exp(-V) dλ = 1 is made. On the integrable branch, μ has density proportional to exp(-V) against λ, and R_y has density proportional to exp(-V(x)-‖x-y‖²/(2η)) against λ. The gradient ∇ of any scalar function is its real Hilbert Riesz representative of the Fréchet derivative; all vector integrals below are actual Bochner integrals.\nThe theorem asserts jointly that μ is a probability measure, Vη is C², and, for every y ∈ E, R_y is a probability measure, the identity function x ↦ x is in L²(R_y), ∇V is Bochner integrable with respect to R_y, and\n    ∇Vη(y) = ∫_E ∇V(x) R_y(dx).\nIn addition, there exists one measurable function p : E → E such that, for every y ∈ E,\n    p(y) + η ∇V(p(y)) = y.\nFor this same function p, define for all y, u ∈ E\n    ρ_y(u) = V(p(y)+s u) - V(p(y)) - s ⟨∇V(p(y)),u⟩,\n    r_y = (x ↦ s⁻¹(x-p(y)))_# R_y,\nwhere # denotes the actual measurable pushforward. Then, for every y ∈ E, all of the following hold jointly:\n    r_y = λ.tilted(u ↦ -(‖u‖²/2 + ρ_y(u))),\n    r_y is a probability measure,\n    ∇ρ_y ∈ L²(r_y),\n    ∇Vη(y) = ∇V(p(y)) + s⁻¹ ∫_E ∇ρ_y(u) r_y(du),\nand, for every z ∈ E,\n    ∇V(p(y)+s z) = ∇V(p(y)) + s⁻¹ ∇ρ_y(z).\nHere multiplication of a scalar and an E-valued vector is real scalar multiplication, and ∇ρ_y differentiates with respect to u with y fixed. The moment, selector, smoothness and mean identities are outputs, not supplied premises. η is any positive real number, without a cap or relation to κ. All displayed conclusions use this fixed input η and the same produced function p; the last identity quantifies over every z for each y.\nDimension zero is included: d = 0, C = 1, E is the singleton zero vector space, A(0) = exp(-V(0)), Vη(0) = V(0), p(0) = 0, ρ_0(0) = 0, all gradients and vector integrals are zero, and μ, R_0 and r_0 are the singleton probability measure. No strictly positive dimension is assumed.",
